WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.590
There's been a big turnout for early voting
in the US, with long lines at many polling

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.750
stations like this one in Los Angeles County.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.959
But one group was noticeably absent, as many
election volunteers noticed.

00:00:12.959 --> 00:00:17.100
I haven’t seen a lot of Latinos today.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.600
That's come as a surprise --since one Latin-American
issue has played a big role in the campaign:

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:28.220
the migrants making their way to the US-Mexican
border from Central America.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.090
President Trump has put the issue of illegal
migration at the forefront of his campaigning

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:38.449
for the Republicans -- blaming the Democrats
for allowing them into the United States.

00:00:38.449 --> 00:00:43.429
Democrats hope to turn Latino discontent over
Trump’s migration rhetoric into votes.

00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:48.019
But they don't have a detailed strategy for
the diverse Latino community, according to

00:00:48.019 --> 00:00:54.530
Sonja Diaz, the head of a Latino think tank.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:01:02.019
Almost 60% of Latinos surveyed in a national
poll had not received any sort of information

00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:04.570
or communication from a candidate.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.119
Latino interest groups are trying to fill
the void.

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:13.860
Many, such as the immigrants’ rights organization
CHIRLA in Los Angeles, have set up call centers.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.310
Volunteers have translated voter guides and
are reaching out to potential voters.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:23.170
Six out of 10 Latinos are millennials, and
many are voting for the first time.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.270
The latest polls have shown that less than
half of them know who’s running in their

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.300
respective districts.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.630
With the political rhetoric high, and a growing
distrust in the government and its institutions,

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.159
many have simply tuned out.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:47.170
"The Trump administration has had a very negative
affect on the way Latinos view the elections

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.680
because we now believe that our vote does
not count or it's not going to affect the

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.159
administration."

00:01:55.159 --> 00:01:58.920
Kevin de León is a Democratic candidate for
Senator.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.200
He's been one of the most visible of all the
Latino candidates in California.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.119
And he's been urging Democrats to articulate
a position beyond “Stop Trump”.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.970
During a campaign rally at his favorite Taco
vendor, De Leon called on Latinos to vote

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.489
for his fellow Democrats to help win back
the House of Representatives.

00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:22.480
It’s critical that in these very dangerous
times in our nation’s history that we engage

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.620
in the battle for the soul of our nation against
a president without one, that the Latino vote

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.542
comes out strong, that come Tuesday November
6 we send a very clear message that we are

00:02:32.542 --> 00:02:34.700
one America.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.510
Latino voters represent one of the fastest
growing demographics in the United States.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.650
This year's midterms may indicate whether
the Democrats have found the key to mobilising

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:44.870
them at the polls.

